<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <!--
      A warning nobody reads is not a warning. M5 proved that concretely: ASPDEPR005
      (ForwardedHeadersOptions.KnownNetworks is obsolete in .NET 10) reached main on a
      line written in the same PR that reported "0 warnings" - the local build was
      incremental and never recompiled the file, and CI printed the warning into output
      no human reads. It surfaced days later, by accident, because a container image
      build compiles from scratch by construction.

      Turned on when the solution emitted zero warnings, so it cost nothing at the point
      of adoption. That is the only cheap moment to do this: on a codebase with a warning
      backlog the same change is a migration.

      The real cost is deferred and worth stating plainly. A future SDK or analyser
      release can introduce a new warning and turn a green build red without any change
      to this repository - the build breaks on someone else's schedule. That is accepted
      here, because the alternative is the failure above, and because a deprecation is
      most cheaply fixed on the day it is announced rather than on the day it is removed.

      When it does happen: fix the warning, or suppress that specific code with a comment
      saying why. Do not set this back to false to get a build green - that trades one
      visible failure for every future silent one.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      NuGet audits restore against the GitHub Advisory Database. By default a vulnerable
      package is a warning, which means it is a line of build output nobody reads.
      Promote the four audit codes to errors so a vulnerable dependency stops the build
      here and in CI. NU1901-1904 = low/moderate/high/critical severity advisories.
    -->
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
